<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Gustav Jung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as C. G. Jung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Gustav Jung was a Swiss psychiatrist and the founder of analytical psychology. An early collaborator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, he broke with him in 1913 and developed his own ideas of the collective unconscious, archetypes and the introverted and extraverted personality types. He worked mainly in Zurich and Küsnacht, and his ideas influenced psychology, religious studies and the arts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-gustav-jung.jpg" alt="Carl Gustav Jung" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unbekannt · PDM-owner · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ETH-BIB-Jung,_Carl_Gustav_(1875-1961)-Portrait-Portr_14163_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Gustav Jung was born on 26 July 1875 in Kesswil, in the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a>, the first surviving son of Paul Achilles Jung, a rural pastor in the Swiss Reformed Church, and Emilie Jung, née Preiswerk. His grandfather and namesake was a noted physician and professor of medicine at Basel. In 1903 Jung married Emma Rauschenbach, daughter of the owner of IWC Schaffhausen, whose shares in the watchmaking firm ensured the family's financial security. Emma became his assistant at Burghölzli and a noted psychoanalyst.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jung worked as a research scientist at the Burghölzli psychiatric hospital in Zurich under Eugen Bleuler. He sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Freud</a> his Studies in Word Association in 1906. Freud saw the younger man as his heir, and secured his appointment as president of the International Psychoanalytical Association. But Jung's research and personal vision made it hard to follow the older man's doctrine, and they parted ways in 1913. The division was painful, and Jung went through a period of isolation, describing a confrontation with the unconscious. This led to his Red Book, published partially and posthumously in 2009.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served as an army doctor and became commandant of an internment camp for British soldiers. He emerged with Psychological Types (1921), one of his most influential books, and in 1922 bought land at Bollingen, where he built a tower. Statements he made in the 1930s have been cited as evidence of both contempt and sympathy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Jung founded analytical psychology. Its central concept is individuation, the lifelong process by which the self is differentiated from conscious and unconscious elements, which he considered the main task of human development. He distinguished the personal unconscious from the collective unconscious, shared by all people, and described archetypes as universal and recurring mental images or themes. His typology of extraversion and introversion grew from early word-association experiments. He died on 6 June 1961.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Jung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zurich saw the birth of Dada at the Cabaret Voltaire in 1916, and Swiss authors such as Gottfried Keller, Max Frisch and Friedrich Dürrenmatt, the psychiatrist <b>Carl Gustav Jung</b> and the architect Le Corbusier gained wide reputations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kesswil, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Küsnacht, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.59,9.31&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Kesswil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Carl Gustav Jung"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5535 6015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5535 6015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.59,9.31&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
